{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Research Translation for Learning Design","title":"Telemetry and Privacy Boundaries","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting telemetry and privacy boundaries to storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Apply data minimization: derive the logging plan from a stated question rather than from technical capability.","Distinguish direct identifiers, indirect identifiers, and pseudonymous keys, and explain how combinations enable re-identification.","Set purpose limitation and a retention window that match the consent learners actually gave.","Reject \"collect now, decide later\" requests and state the harm they create."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"03","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/07/02"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_calibration"]},"content":"# Telemetry and Privacy Boundaries\n\n![Telemetry and Privacy Boundaries textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-03-telemetry-and-privacy-boundaries.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA prototype that logs learner behavior is collecting personal data, whether or not the designer thinks of it that way. The default temptation is to capture everything (\"we might want it later\"), but that default inverts the ethical and legal order: data collection should follow from a question, not precede it. This section teaches data minimization as a design discipline. The principle, central to FERPA's protection of education records and to the GDPR notion of purpose limitation, is that you collect the smallest amount of the least identifiable data needed to answer a stated question, and you bind its use and retention to what learners agreed to.\n\nFor a learning designer, telemetry is not an IT afterthought. The fields you choose to log, the granularity, the identifiers, and the retention window are design decisions with consequences for re-identification risk, learner trust, and what claims you are later allowed to make. Getting the boundary right is part of designing the artifact, not a checkbox after it ships.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Apply data minimization: derive the logging plan from a stated question rather than from technical capability.\n- Distinguish direct identifiers, indirect identifiers, and pseudonymous keys, and explain how combinations enable re-identification.\n- Set purpose limitation and a retention window that match the consent learners actually gave.\n- Reject \"collect now, decide later\" requests and state the harm they create.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team is excited about the analytics dashboard their prototype could produce, so the first logging plan is: capture the full clickstream, keystroke timing, screen recordings, and each learner's name and email, stored indefinitely \"so we can analyze it later.\" Nothing here is tied to a question. Names and email maximize identifiability; screen recordings can capture incidental third parties; indefinite storage means the data outlives any purpose and any consent. The plan feels thorough, but thoroughness in collection is precisely the risk. The work of this section is to replace \"log everything\" with \"log what one question needs, and no more.\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTelemetry design turns on four boundaries, each answering a different question:\n\n- **Purpose:** Which single, stated design question does this field help answer? A field with no question behind it has no justification.\n- **Minimization:** What is the smallest data that answers the question at the lowest identifiability? Often a counter or a timestamp suffices where a recording was proposed.\n- **Identifiability:** Is the key a direct identifier (name, email), an indirect identifier (a quasi-identifier like timestamp + class + device that can be triangulated), or a pseudonymous random id? Re-identification usually happens not through one field but through the *combination* of several, so minimizing fields lowers risk multiplicatively, not additively.\n- **Retention and use:** How long is the data kept, and is its use bound to the consent given? Indefinite retention and scope creep (\"we collected it for X, now we use it for Y\") are the two most common boundary violations.\n\nThe course anchors connect privacy to learning quality, not just compliance. **Cognitive load theory** is relevant because intrusive data capture (a visible recorder, a consent wall that reappears) can itself add extraneous load and alter the very behavior you are trying to measure. **UDL learner variability** matters because identifiability harms are unevenly distributed: a small cohort, a learner from a marginalized group, or a class small enough that \"the only student who took it on a phone\" is identifiable, all bear more re-identification risk from the same fields. **Mayer's principles** remind you that telemetry should serve a learning question; data with no link to a learning decision is collection for its own sake.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Direct identifier\",\"definition\":\"A field that names the learner on its own, such as full name or email; maximizes identifiability and should be dropped unless a stated question needs it.\"},{\"term\":\"Indirect (quasi) identifier\",\"definition\":\"A field harmless alone but re-identifying in combination, such as timestamp plus class plus device, which can triangulate one learner.\"},{\"term\":\"Pseudonymous key\",\"definition\":\"A random per-study id with no meaning outside the dataset; lets you link the records of one learner without storing who they are.\"},{\"term\":\"Purpose limitation\",\"definition\":\"Binding the use and retention of a field to the single stated question and the consent learners actually gave, blocking scope creep.\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"In a four-person pilot, you log only response time, device type, and completion order, with no names. Why might this still re-identify a learner?\" explanation=\"Re-identification usually lives in the join, not a single field. In a four-person cohort, response time plus device plus completion order can triangulate to one learner even though no direct identifier is stored.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It cannot; removing names anonymizes the dataset.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The combination of quasi-identifiers in a tiny cohort can single out one learner.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Response time is a direct identifier.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTrace one logging plan from over-collection to a defensible boundary.\n\n**Before (logging plan, first draft):**\n\n> Log everything the prototype can capture: full clickstream, keystroke timing, screen recording, and each learner's name and email, stored indefinitely so we can analyze it later.\n\n1. No field is tied to a stated question, so none can be justified.\n2. Name plus email are direct identifiers; combined with timestamps they make every record trivially re-identifiable.\n3. Indefinite retention sets no purpose limit, so the data outlives any consent.\n\n**After (minimized logging plan):**\n\n> Question: does the new hint button reduce time-to-correct on item 4? Log only item-4 response time and hint-click events, keyed to a random pseudonymous id, retained for the study term and used only to improve the lesson, as stated in the consent form.\n\nThe revised plan collects exactly two fields the question needs, drops direct identifiers in favor of a random key, and binds use and retention to the consent learners gave. A reviewer can see the boundary and check it against the consent text.\n\nOne rejected request: a teammate asked to also store email \"for follow-up.\" I declined because no current question requires it; adding email would reintroduce a direct identifier and widen re-identification risk with no design justification. If a follow-up study is later approved, it can seek its own consent rather than borrowing this one.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDerive the plan from the question, then test each proposed field against the four boundaries:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Which stated question does this field answer? No question, no field.\n2. **Minimization check:** Is there a less identifiable field that answers the same question (a count instead of a recording, a pseudonymous key instead of a name)?\n3. **Combination check:** Could this field, joined with the others, re-identify a learner in the smallest plausible cohort? If so, coarsen or drop it.\n4. **Retention-and-use check:** Does the retention window end when the purpose ends, and is use confined to the consent given?\n\nA field that fails any check is not a \"nice to have\"; it is an uncompensated risk to the learner.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Resolve a collect-now-decide-later request\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your question is: does the new hint button reduce time-to-correct on item 4? A teammate says: \\\"While we are at it, log full clickstream, screen recordings, and each learner email, stored indefinitely, so we can analyze it later.\\\" What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Agree; more data now means more options later.\",\"next\":\"hoard\",\"feedback\":\"Collection that precedes a question inverts the order. Each field is an uncompensated risk.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the purpose check: which stated question does each field answer?\",\"next\":\"purpose\",\"feedback\":\"Right. No question, no field.\"}]},\"hoard\":{\"prompt\":\"Months later a breach exposes emails and recordings that answered no question and outlived the consent. Back up: what should you have asked of each field?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Which stated question does it answer?\",\"next\":\"purpose\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly the check that would have blocked the over-collection.\"}]},\"purpose\":{\"prompt\":\"Only item-4 response time and hint-click events answer the question. The teammate still wants email \\\"for follow-up.\\\" How do you respond?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep email; a future study might need it.\",\"next\":\"email\",\"feedback\":\"No current question requires it; adding email reintroduces a direct identifier with no justification.\"},{\"label\":\"Decline email and key the two needed fields to a random pseudonymous id.\",\"next\":\"minimize\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. A later approved study can seek its own consent.\"}]},\"email\":{\"prompt\":\"Storing email widens re-identification risk for a benefit no current question needs. What is the minimal defensible plan?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Log item-4 time and hint clicks under a random id, retained for the study term.\",\"next\":\"minimize\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, this matches collection to the question and the consent.\"}]},\"minimize\":{\"prompt\":\"Final plan: two fields the question needs, a pseudonymous key instead of names, retention bound to the study term and use bound to the consent. A reviewer can check the boundary against the consent text.\",\"feedback\":\"You derived the plan from the question, not from technical capability.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is that anonymization is a switch you flip after collection. Removing names does not anonymize a dataset if quasi-identifiers remain; a record keyed by class, date, and device can still single out one learner. A second misreading treats consent as a blanket permission. Consent is scoped: data gathered \"to improve this lesson\" cannot be quietly repurposed for a marketing model or a different study. Boundary work is about matching collection, identifiability, retention, and use to the specific purpose and the specific consent.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the boundaries to a discussion-board prototype. A first plan logs every post's full text, author name, IP address, and timestamp, kept forever, to study engagement. Walk the checks. Purpose: the question is \"does a prompt scaffold raise the rate of evidence-based replies?\" That needs a coded reply-type per post, not the full verbatim text plus identifiers. Minimization: replace stored full text with a derived code (evidence-based: yes/no) computed at collection time; drop IP entirely. Combination: author name plus timestamp in a 12-person seminar re-identifies everyone, so swap names for a random per-study id. Retention: keep coded counts for the study term, then delete. The revised plan answers the engagement question while collecting a fraction of the data and almost none of the identifiability, which is exactly the trade data minimization is designed to produce.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nFor each logged field, write two things: the question it serves and the worst-case re-identification it enables in the smallest cohort you can imagine. A strong reader notices that the danger usually lives in the join, not the field: response-time alone is harmless, but response-time plus device plus a four-person class is not. The skill is to read a logging plan adversarially, as an attacker who wants to single out one learner, and to keep only the fields that survive that reading while still answering the question.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe telemetry plan is strong when every field traces to a stated question, when identifiers are minimized to the lowest level the question allows, and when the README records the retention window and the consent scope alongside the field list. A reviewer should be able to see, for at least one field you dropped, the re-identification risk that justified dropping it. Treat the dashboard's visual appeal as the last concern; the load-bearing layer is the field-by-field justification against the four boundaries.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the plan into a stricter jurisdiction or a smaller cohort, the two constraints that most often break a telemetry design. In a five-student pilot, fields that were safe in a 200-student deployment (timestamp, device, completion order) may now re-identify everyone, so the same question forces a coarser plan: bucket timestamps, drop device, report only aggregates. The purpose stays stable while the identifiability boundary tightens. Testing your plan against the smallest, most regulated context is how you confirm you understand minimization as a relationship between data and cohort, not as a fixed list of \"safe\" fields.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-03-telemetry-and-privacy-boundaries-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 07.03. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes records cognitive-load diagnosis, support request, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","stem":"Your prototype can log many learner events. Which logging plan best reflects data minimization tied to a stated design question?","options":["Capture everything available (keystrokes, full screen video, location) in case it is useful later.","Log only the events your study question needs, such as time-on-task per item, and store them under a pseudonymous id rather than a name.","Log nothing, because any telemetry violates privacy.","Log full names alongside answers so the data is easy to read."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Minimization means collecting only what a specific question requires and reducing identifiability. Capturing everything or naming learners expands risk with no design justification.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_collect_everything","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","stem":"A teammate says, \"We removed names, so the telemetry is fully anonymous.\" Why might this be wrong?","options":["It is correct; removing names always anonymizes data.","Combining timestamps, section, and rare answer patterns can still re-identify a learner, so removing the name alone does not guarantee anonymity.","Anonymity does not matter for prototypes.","The data should not have been collected at all."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"De-identification is not the same as anonymization. Quasi-identifiers in combination can single out an individual even without a name.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_deid_equals_anon","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You consider sending raw learner telemetry to an external AI service to summarize behavior. What is the defensible boundary?","options":["Send all raw logs with identifiers, since the AI gives better summaries with more data.","Send only the minimized, pseudonymous fields your consent language covers, and confirm the service does not retain them beyond the stated purpose.","Never use any AI on learner data under any circumstance.","Send the data and decide about consent afterward if anyone asks."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The boundary is set by consent scope, minimization, and retention. The tool may help, but only on data the disclosure covers and only with controlled retention.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_consent_after_the_fact","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","stem":"Your consent form promised data only for improving the lesson. Mid-study, a colleague asks to reuse the logs for a separate marketing model. What is the principled response?","options":["Share it; the data already exists, so reuse is free.","Decline or seek fresh consent, because the new purpose falls outside the boundary learners agreed to.","Share it but remove names first, which makes any reuse acceptable.","Delete all data to avoid the question."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Purpose limitation binds use to what learners consented to. A new purpose requires new consent, not just de-identification.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_consent_after_the_fact","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_collect_everything","pattern":"collect_everything_just_in_case","description":"Logging the maximum available telemetry with no link to a specific design question, on the assumption that more data is always safer for the project","trigger":"learner proposes capturing all events, video, or location without naming the question each field answers","feedback":"Tie every logged field to a question it answers. Data you cannot justify is risk you cannot justify.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_deid_equals_anon","pattern":"deidentification_equals_anonymization","description":"Assuming that removing names makes telemetry anonymous, ignoring re-identification from combined quasi-identifiers","trigger":"learner claims the data is anonymous solely because direct identifiers were dropped","feedback":"Check whether timestamps, section, and rare patterns could single someone out. Removing the name is necessary but not sufficient.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_3_telemetry_and_privacy_boundaries_consent_after_the_fact","pattern":"consent_treated_as_retroactive","description":"Treating consent as something that can be obtained after data is reused, or as covering any later purpose once data exists","trigger":"learner reuses logs for a purpose outside the original disclosure, or plans to ask permission only if challenged","feedback":"Bind use to the purpose learners agreed to. A new purpose needs new consent before the data moves.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"72c5532fc3f82de2abbf7f15766aed0060e8ff836049311576354692cd128326","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}